  { q: 'Is seeing a raven in a dream good or bad?', a: 'Whether a raven in a dream is a good or bad sign depends entirely on the interpretive framework applied. In traditions that view ravens positively — as messengers of wisdom, divine companions, or creator figures — a raven in a dream may carry positive meaning. In traditions that link ravens to death and ill omen, the same dream may be interpreted negatively. Modern psychological approaches (Jungian and others) would treat the raven as a symbol for the dreamer\'s unconscious shadow or hidden knowledge, which is neither inherently good nor bad. No universal meaning exists.' },
  { q: 'What does it mean to dream of a talking raven?', a: 'A talking raven in a dream is a potent image in many cultural frameworks — appropriate given that real ravens can mimic speech and that mythological ravens (Huginn and Muninn, Apollo\'s raven) are explicitly messengers and news-carriers. In Jungian analysis, a talking animal may represent the voice of the unconscious or shadow self. In traditional Indigenous North American frameworks, a speaking Raven figure in a dream might be interpreted as a visitation by the Trickster-Creator or as spirit communication. The specific message in the dream, and the emotions associated with it, would typically be considered more significant than the raven\'s presence alone.' },
  { q: 'What does a dead raven in a dream mean?', a: 'Dream interpretation of a dead raven varies by tradition. In Western folk tradition, a dead raven (itself a death omen) might be interpreted as the end of a bad period — the death of the death-bringer. In some psychological approaches, a dead raven might represent the integration or resolution of a shadowy unconscious element. In lore traditions where ravens are associated with specific gods (Odin, Apollo), a dead raven might be interpreted as a warning of divine disfavour. As with all dream interpretation, the dreamer\'s personal associations and emotional response within the dream context are considered primary in psychological frameworks.' },
];
